-- allium: 3
-- quantile_sketch.allium

-- Scope: how the Agent's distribution sketch (pkg/util/quantile) turns
--   observations into bins: what an insert, a merge and a reset do to the
--   bins, how many bins they take, how the bin limit trims them, and what
--   quantiles and serialised columns are read back from them.
-- Includes: the bin budget (bin limit, bin width, max count), the Agent-side
--   inserts (unsampled values, sampled values, interpolated histogram
--   buckets) and the batches they hand to the bin store, overflow bins,
--   trimming, scaling past max count and the remainders it keeps, merge,
--   reset, the summary, quantiles, equality and the payload columns handed
--   to the serializer.
-- Excludes:
--   - The value-to-key mapping (gamma, bias, saturation to the infinite
--     keys). Keys are ordered integers; key_of, finite_key and key_value are
--     black boxes.
--   - Non-finite values and bounds. The aggregator drops them before they
--     reach an AgentSketch; see the open questions for what happens if they
--     do.
--   - How InsertInterpolate spreads a bucket's count over its keys: the
--     interpolate black box only promises a total and key order.
--   - Buffer pooling, printing and byte-level memory accounting. Memory is
--     modelled as the number of bins a store holds.
--   - The DDSketch conversion, beyond the batch of rounded counts it hands
--     to InsertBatch on a new store.
--   - What callers do around the sketch: the OTLP mapper drops data points
--     past max_count and overwrites the summary of the sketches it builds;
--     the serializer leaves avg out of V3 payloads and drops sketches too
--     big for a payload.
--
-- Black box functions:
--   key_of(value)               the key of the bin a finite value falls into.
--                               Keys are ordered like values and symmetric
--                               around 0, with an infinite key past the
--                               largest finite one on each side.
--   finite_key(value)           key_of(value), with the infinite keys
--                               clamped to the largest finite ones.
--   key_value(key)              the value a key stands for: the middle of its
--                               bin on a log scale, so that
--                               key_of(key_value(k)) = k. Infinite at the
--                               infinite keys.
--   tally(keys)                 one KeyCount per distinct key, in key order.
--   one_key(key, count)         a batch of a single KeyCount.
--   interpolate(lower, upper, count)
--                               count spread over the keys from
--                               finite_key(lower) to finite_key(upper), in
--                               key order, at most one KeyCount per key,
--                               adding up to count. Nothing for a count past
--                               2^63 - 1, see the open questions.
--   total(batch)                the sum of a batch's counts.
--   total_bins(bins)            the sum of the bins' counts.
--   sorted_by_key(bins)         whether keys never decrease along bins.
--   add_counts(bins, batch)     the bins with each KeyCount of the batch,
--                               whose keys are distinct, added in key order.
--                               The count joins the first bin of its key, or
--                               a new bin if there is none. Their sum n
--                               becomes a bin of n mod max_bin_width, left
--                               out if empty, followed by n div
--                               max_bin_width full bins. The other bins of
--                               the key stay as they are. So the bins grow
--                               with the count, not only with the keys, and
--                               a key can hold several partial bins.
--   add_bins(bins, other)       the bins of another store added, walking
--                               both in key order: the i-th bin of a key in
--                               other joins the i-th bin of that key in
--                               bins, their sum split as in add_counts, or
--                               stays as it is when bins has fewer bins of
--                               that key.
--   trim_left(bins, limit)      bins unchanged if there are no more than
--                               limit of them. Otherwise the lowest
--                               (bins.count - limit) bins are emptied, lowest
--                               first, into a running carry that ends in the
--                               lowest kept bin. Each time the carry passes
--                               max_bin_width after taking a bin in, a full
--                               bin stays behind at that bin's key and the
--                               carry drops by max_bin_width. What the
--                               lowest kept bin cannot hold of the rest goes
--                               into one more bin of its key, just before
--                               it. The result is those overflow bins, then
--                               the top limit bins: the total and the key
--                               order hold, but observations move to higher
--                               keys. It can hold more than limit bins, so
--                               the next trim_left folds again.
--   record(summary, value, weight)
--                               the summary with weight more observations
--                               of value: count grows by truncate(weight),
--                               sum by weight * value, min and max take
--                               value in, and avg moves toward value by
--                               truncate(weight) / count. So avg can differ
--                               from sum / count.
--   record_deposits(summary, deposits)
--                               record(summary, key_value(d.key), d.count)
--                               for each deposit d.
--   merged(summary, other)      the summary of both sets of observations.
--   value_at_rank(bins, rank, summary)
--                               walking the bins in order, the first one
--                               whose cumulative count passes rank. The
--                               result moves (rank - the count before that
--                               bin) / its count of the way from its
--                               key_value (summary.min for the first bin)
--                               toward key_value * gamma, the ratio between
--                               the values of consecutive positive keys.
--                               That is the next key's value for a positive
--                               key, but the previous key's for a negative
--                               one, 0 for key 0 and infinite at the
--                               infinite keys: the result can fall outside
--                               summary.min and summary.max, or be NaN, see
--                               the open questions. A NaN rank gives NaN;
--                               summary.max if no bin passes the rank.
--   fitting_shift(store, batch) the smallest shift, no lower than the
--                               store's and at most max_shift, at which the
--                               store's units and the batch's fit max_count.
--                               It counts every count divided by the unit,
--                               plus a unit for every remainder and batch
--                               entry and one more, for what remainders can
--                               make up. An unscaled store stays at 0 while
--                               count plus total(batch) fits.
--   fitting_merge_shift(store, other)
--                               the same, for the units of both stores, no
--                               lower than either shift.
--   rescale(store, shift)       the store's holdings at shift: each key's
--                               units divided by 2^(shift - store.shift),
--                               rounded down, its bins merged. What a key's
--                               units lose goes to its remainder.
--   carry(remainders, incoming, shift, limit)
--                               for each key, its remainder and incoming
--                               observations make up the floor of their sum
--                               over 2^shift in units, and the rest is its new
--                               remainder. So the units a key gains do not
--                               depend on how its observations were batched.
--                               Past limit keys, the lowest remainders fold
--                               into the next key holding one.
--   as_counts(remainders)       the remainders, as KeyCounts.
--   total_remainders(remainders)
--                               the sum of the remainders' observations.
--   scale_columns(bins, unit)   the bins, with their counts times unit.
--   power_of_two(n), min(a, b)
--   round_half_even(x), truncate(x), append(list, item)
--   empty()                     an empty list.
--   empty_summary()             the summary of no observations, all zeros.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The payload builder that reads finished sketches.
external entity Serializer {}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value Summary {
    count: Integer
    sum: Decimal
    min: Decimal
    max: Decimal
    avg: Decimal
}

-- A bin counts units of one key: observations, until the store scales. A key
-- with more than max_bin_width units spans several bins, which stay next to
-- each other.
value Bin {
    key: Integer
    count: Integer
}

-- Observations of one key, as handed to a store in a batch.
value KeyCount {
    key: Integer
    count: Integer
}

-- Observations of one key that fall short of a unit of a scaled store.
value Remainder {
    key: Integer
    observations: Integer
}

-- A store's bins and remainders, at some shift.
value Holdings {
    bins: List<Bin>
    count: Integer
    remainders: List<Remainder>
}

-- What carry makes of remainders and incoming observations.
value Carry {
    units: List<KeyCount>
    remainders: List<Remainder>
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

entity BinStore {
    bins: List<Bin>
    -- The units held in the bins.
    count: Integer
    -- Each unit stands for 2^shift observations. 0 until the store is given
    -- more than max_count.
    shift: Integer
    -- Sorted by key, at most one per key. Empty while shift is 0.
    remainders: List<Remainder>

    unit: power_of_two(shift)
    observations: count * unit + total_remainders(remainders)

    invariant CountMatchesBins {
        count = total_bins(bins)
    }

    invariant BinsSortedByKey {
        sorted_by_key(bins)
    }

    invariant BinsWithinWidth {
        bins.all(b => b.count <= config.max_bin_width)
    }

    -- Each bin holds at most max_bin_width units, so the bins grow at least
    -- as fast as the count. Scaling keeps the count within max_count.
    invariant BinsGrowWithCount {
        bins.count * config.max_bin_width >= count
    }

    -- Unless the shift tops out at max_shift, past 2^63 times max_count
    -- observations.
    invariant UnitsWithinMaxCount {
        shift < config.max_shift implies count <= config.max_count
    }

    -- bin_limit bins, plus at most bin_limit + 1 overflow bins that trim_left
    -- cannot fold within max_count units.
    invariant BinsWithinBudget {
        shift < config.max_shift implies bins.count <= 2 * config.bin_limit + 1
    }

    invariant RemaindersBelowUnit {
        remainders.all(r => r.observations > 0 and r.observations < unit)
    }

    invariant RemaindersWithinBinLimit {
        remainders.count <= config.bin_limit
    }
}

entity Sketch {
    store: BinStore
    -- Updated by every insert, whatever happens to the bins.
    summary: Summary

    -- Ranks are counted in units; the remainders, under a unit per key, are
    -- left out.
    quantile(q):
        if store.count = 0: 0
        else if q <= 0: summary.min
        else if q >= 1: summary.max
        else: value_at_rank(store.bins, round_half_even(q * (store.count - 1)), summary)

    -- As the code compares sketches: by float equality, so a summary holding
    -- NaN is same_as nothing, its own copy included.
    same_as(other):
        summary = other.summary
        and store.count = other.store.count
        and store.bins = other.store.bins
        and store.shift = other.store.shift
        and store.remainders = other.store.remainders

    -- What the serializer reads, one column per bin, scaled back to
    -- observations as far as the payload's 32-bit counts allow.
    columns: scale_columns(store.bins, power_of_two(min(store.shift, config.max_column_shift)))
}

-- The insert-optimised sketch the aggregator keeps per context and bucket
-- (quantile.Agent). Unsampled values wait in a buffer before reaching the
-- bins; the summary records them at once.
entity AgentSketch {
    sketch: Sketch
    buffered_keys: List<Integer>

    is_empty: sketch.summary.count = 0 and buffered_keys.count = 0
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Bins trim_left keeps besides the overflow bins it cannot fold.
    -- NewConfig turns 0 into this default.
    bin_limit: Integer = 4096
    -- Units one bin can count.
    max_bin_width: Integer = 65_535
    -- The most units the bins of a store count. Past it, the store scales.
    max_count: Integer = bin_limit * max_bin_width
    -- Unsampled values buffered before they reach the bins.
    agent_buffer_capacity: Integer = 512
    -- The largest shift, so that a unit fits 64 bits.
    max_shift: Integer = 63
    -- The largest shift at which a full bin, scaled back to observations,
    -- still fits a 32-bit payload count.
    max_column_shift: Integer = 16
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A sample rate outside (0, 1], NaN included, counts as unsampled.
rule AgentRecordsUnsampledValue {
    when: InsertValue(agent, value, sample_rate)
    requires: not (sample_rate > 0 and sample_rate < 1)

    ensures: agent.sketch.summary = record(agent.sketch.summary, value, 1)
    ensures: agent.buffered_keys = append(agent.buffered_keys, key_of(value))
    ensures:
        if agent.buffered_keys.count >= config.agent_buffer_capacity:
            FlushAgent(agent)
}

-- A sampled value stands for 1 / sample_rate observations of it, truncated.
rule AgentRecordsSampledValue {
    when: InsertValue(agent, value, sample_rate)
    requires: sample_rate > 0 and sample_rate < 1

    -- A float64 quotient: @1e-9 weighs 999,999,999.
    let weight = 1 / sample_rate

    ensures: agent.sketch.summary = record(agent.sketch.summary, value, weight)
    ensures: FlushAgent(agent)
    ensures: InsertBatch(agent.sketch.store, one_key(key_of(value), truncate(weight)))

    @guidance
        -- The buffered keys reach the bins before this batch. The order
        -- matters once trim_left folds bins, or the store scales.
}

-- A histogram bucket's count, spread over the keys between its bounds. Finite
-- bounds past the largest finite key count as the largest finite key, on
-- either side.
rule AgentInterpolatesBucket {
    when: InsertInterpolate(agent, lower, upper, count)
    -- Otherwise the insert fails with non-monotonic boundaries.
    requires: finite_key(upper) >= finite_key(lower)

    let deposits = interpolate(lower, upper, count)

    ensures: agent.sketch.summary = record_deposits(agent.sketch.summary, deposits)
    ensures: FlushAgent(agent)
    ensures:
        if deposits.count > 0:
            InsertBatch(agent.sketch.store, deposits)

    @guidance
        -- The buffered keys reach the bins before the deposits, as for a
        -- sampled value.
}

rule AgentFlushes {
    when: FlushAgent(agent)
    requires: agent.buffered_keys.count > 0

    -- Bound before the buffer is cleared: emitted parameters read the
    -- resulting state.
    let batch = tally(agent.buffered_keys)

    ensures: InsertBatch(agent.sketch.store, batch)
    ensures: agent.buffered_keys = empty()
}

-- Past max_count units, the store halves its unit as many times as it takes
-- for the batch to fit, rather than let the bins grow with the count.
rule StoreInsertsBatch {
    when: InsertBatch(store, batch)

    let shift = fitting_shift(store, batch)
    let rescaled = rescale(store, shift)
    let carried = carry(rescaled.remainders, batch, shift, config.bin_limit)

    ensures: store.shift = shift
    ensures: store.bins = trim_left(add_counts(rescaled.bins, carried.units), config.bin_limit)
    ensures: store.count = rescaled.count + total(carried.units)
    ensures: store.remainders = carried.remainders

    @guidance
        -- Scale before add_counts allocates: past max_count, add_counts would
        -- take a bin per max_bin_width of the batch, and trim_left keeps full
        -- bins. At shift 0 the batch goes to add_counts as it is.
}

-- The other sketch is left as it was: it is rescaled as a copy.
rule SketchMerges {
    when: MergeSketch(sketch, other)

    let shift = fitting_merge_shift(sketch.store, other.store)
    let mine = rescale(sketch.store, shift)
    let theirs = rescale(other.store, shift)
    let carried = carry(mine.remainders, as_counts(theirs.remainders), shift, config.bin_limit)
    let merged_bins = trim_left(add_bins(mine.bins, theirs.bins), config.bin_limit)

    ensures: sketch.summary = merged(sketch.summary, other.summary)
    ensures: sketch.store.shift = shift
    ensures: sketch.store.bins = trim_left(add_counts(merged_bins, carried.units), config.bin_limit)
    ensures: sketch.store.count = mine.count + theirs.count + total(carried.units)
    ensures: sketch.store.remainders = carried.remainders
}

rule AgentResets {
    when: ResetSketch(agent)

    ensures: agent.sketch.summary = empty_summary()
    ensures: agent.sketch.store.bins = empty()
    ensures: agent.sketch.store.count = 0
    ensures: agent.sketch.store.shift = 0
    ensures: agent.sketch.store.remainders = empty()
    ensures: agent.buffered_keys = empty()
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- No observation is lost on the way to the bins, as long as weights and
-- counts stay below 2^63: see the open questions.
invariant SummaryCountsEveryObservation {
    for a in AgentSketches:
        a.sketch.summary.count = a.sketch.store.observations + a.buffered_keys.count
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The serializer reads the sketches AgentSketches finish, and sketches
-- converted from a DDSketch. Finishing flushes the buffer, then hands over a
-- copy of the sketch, or nothing when the AgentSketch is_empty.
surface SketchPayload {
    facing serializer: Serializer

    context sketch: Sketch

    exposes:
        sketch.summary.count
        sketch.summary.sum
        sketch.summary.min
        sketch.summary.max
        sketch.summary.avg
        for c in sketch.columns:
            c.key
            c.count

    @guarantee ColumnsCountEveryObservation
        -- For sketches as this package builds them, up to max_column_shift,
        -- the column counts add up to the observations in the bins: the
        -- summary's count once the buffer is flushed, short of the
        -- remainders, under a unit per key. Past it, the columns keep the
        -- bins' proportions only.

    @guarantee FinishedSketchIsACopy
        -- The finished sketch holds the AgentSketch's summary, count, bins,
        -- shift and remainders once flushed, and shares no bins with it.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "Counts past 2^63: a DogStatsD sample rate at or below 2^-63 (about 1.08e-19) is accepted, and 1 / sample_rate no longer fits the signed 64-bit counts. Go leaves those conversions to the platform: on amd64 the summary count turns negative (two @1e-20 samples wrap it to 0, so Finish drops the sketch), while the store's count differs from it or a bin of 0 appears; arm64 saturates instead. InsertInterpolate with a count past 2^63 - 1 records nothing and reports no error. Should the Agent clamp such weights and counts, reject them, or leave them to callers?"

open question "Non-finite inputs: key_of(+Inf) is 1, the key next to 0, and NaN's key depends on the platform. An AgentSketch given an infinite or NaN value, or such a bucket bound, keeps it in a bin near 0 while its summary holds the infinity or NaN. The aggregator drops these before the Agent sees them, but the package is its own module. Should the Agent reject them itself, or should the spec make finite inputs a precondition?"

open question "Quantiles outside the observations: value_at_rank interpolates toward key_value * gamma, which is the previous key for negative keys. So a quantile can fall below summary.min (from -100 x 4 and -10 x 4, the 10th percentile is -100.38), quantiles can decrease within a bin as q increases, and values above about 4.2e211 give NaN. Is this intended, given that outside tests only printing reads Quantile, or should value_at_rank stay within the bin and within summary.min and summary.max?"

open question "trim_left is not idempotent: a store that overflow bins leave above bin_limit folds more of its lowest bins on every later insert, and the bins depend on how inserts were batched and ordered, even without scaling. Should trim_left fold until the store is back within bin_limit bins, or is re-folding on later inserts acceptable?"
